  "Number": 15,
  "Question": "The largest number by which the expression \\(n^3-n\\) is divisible for all possible integral values of \\(n\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(2\\)",
    "B": "\\(3\\)",
    "C": "\\(4\\)",
    "D": "\\(5\\)",
    "E": "\\(6\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Factoring reveals three consecutive integers, whose product has predictable divisibility:\n\\[\nn^3-n=n(n^2-1)=(n-1)n(n+1).\n\\]\nAmong any three consecutive integers, at least one is even and one is a multiple of \\(3\\). Thus their product is divisible by both \\(2\\) and \\(3\\), hence by their least common multiple, \\(6\\). This reasoning applies to negative integers too. When \\(n=-1,0,1\\), the product is zero, which is also divisible by \\(6\\).\n\nWe must also show that no larger divisor always works. Taking \\(n=2\\) gives\n\\[\nn^3-n=8-2=6.\n\\]\nAny positive integer dividing every value must therefore divide \\(6\\), so it cannot exceed \\(6\\). Since \\(6\\) always works, the largest universal divisor is \\(6\\), choice E.",
